From 06aaab97eb11f334b5fde2479f378aa4023b3d49 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: duncan0k <dev@ark0.si>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 11:40:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 66/78] crypto: vendor slhdsa-c for SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s signature
 verification

Ark-0's M4-S adds an experimental tapscript opcode inside P2MR leaves that
verifies an SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s (FIPS 205) signature. This is the verifier
it will use, on its own, before any consensus code: slhdsa-c at commit
a0fc1ff253930060d0246aebca06c2538eb92b88 (Apache-2.0 OR ISC OR MIT, used
here under MIT), the commit liboqs 0.15.0 integrates, taken unmodified
into src/crypto/slhdsa128s/.

- Only the files the SHA2 parameter sets need are taken: slh_sha2.c
  defines all six SHA2 sets and the larger ones use SHA-512, so
  sha2_512.c comes along, but only slh_dsa_sha2_128s is used. The SHAKE
  sets, Keccak and HashSLH-DSA are left out.
- The library's own portable C SHA-256 is used on every platform. It uses
  no SHA extensions, so every machine runs the same verification code, and
  the validation weight of the opcode is to be measured on that code.
- The four C files get their compile options from src/crypto/CMakeLists.txt
  rather than from core_interface, which also carries Threads' generator
  expressions, -fmacro-prefix-map and C++ workarounds: Core's hardening,
  probed again with the C compiler as Core probes it for C++ (the pair
  "-U_FORTIFY_SOURCE -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=3" as one unit with Core's
  optimization check, -Wstack-protector, -fstack-protector-all,
  -fcf-protection=full, -fstack-clash-protection, -mbranch-protection on
  aarch64; /sdl with MSVC), a rejected unit skipped as Core does, so a
  skipped pair leaves what CMAKE_C_FLAGS defines; the -fsanitize flags of
  sanitize_interface, required when SANITIZERS is set; a warning set of
  their own instead of Core's, whose style warnings the upstream files do
  not follow: -Wall -Wextra -Wvla -Wundef -Wimplicit-fallthrough
  -Wbidi-chars=any and, as one unit, -Wformat -Wformat-security (with
  MSVC /W3 and the warnings Core turns off); and APPEND_CPPFLAGS and
  APPEND_CFLAGS last on their command line, as cmake/secp256k1.cmake
  arranges for libsecp256k1. The probes compile with the same rule, so
  they see the appended flags last too, as Core's C++ probes do.
- The library chooses its byte order with
  "#if __BYTE_ORDER__ == __ORDER_BIG_ENDIAN__", which a C compiler that
  defines neither macro (MSVC) reads as big-endian. slhdsa128s_byte_order.h,
  included before each of its files for this target only, supplies the
  macros on MSVC's little-endian targets (_M_IX86, _M_X64, _M_ARM,
  _M_ARM64), stops the build for any other compiler without them or with
  only some of them, and checks them against CMAKE_C_BYTE_ORDER, which
  must equal CMAKE_CXX_BYTE_ORDER. The vendored files stay unmodified.
  The wrapper includes only slh_dsa.h, which needs no byte order, so it
  has no check of its own: a compiler without those macros (MSVC) no
  longer stops there.
- src/crypto/slhdsa128s.h exposes one function, SLHDSA128SVerify:
  slh_verify of FIPS 205, the external interface of pure SLH-DSA with a
  context string, over a 32-byte message. It is not HashSLH-DSA and not
  the internal interface. It checks the length of the public key, the
  message, the signature and the context string before it calls the
  library, because the vendored slh_verify hashes the first 16 bytes of
  the signature before it checks the signature's length. A counter of
  library calls lets tests show that a wrong length never reaches the
  library, and lets measurements count complete verifications.
- Key generation and signing stay in slh_dsa.c, which is not modified;
  only the unit tests and benchmarks call them.
- bitcoinkernel takes the objects of bitcoin_crypto, and objects do not
  carry their target's link dependencies, so it also takes the objects
  of the two vendored libraries bitcoin_crypto links: bitcoin_slhdsa128s,
  and bitcoin_mldsa44, whose objects it had lacked since the ML-DSA-44
  patches. The static library is then self-contained, libbitcoinkernel.pc
  needs nothing new, and the vendored code keeps its own compile options,
  forced include and hidden visibility.

src/crypto/slhdsa128s/README.md records where every file came from, with
its SHA-256.
